1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,242
This disc of baked clay is about three thousand seven hundred years old.

2
00:00:05,242 --> 00:00:10,151
Both sides are covered in a spiral of tiny pictures, stamped one by one.

3
00:00:10,151 --> 00:00:15,333
And after more than a century, nobody on Earth can read a single word of it.

4
00:00:15,666 --> 00:00:19,066
Some say that is because it was never meant for us.

5
00:00:19,066 --> 00:00:21,800
That it is a message from somewhere else.

6
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:25,266
A disk, made to be read by a machine from the stars.

7
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:34,533
So tonight we open a codebreaker's notebook, and treat the Phaistos Disc the way cryptographers treat an intercepted enemy message.

8
00:00:34,866 --> 00:00:36,219
We log the intercept.

9
00:00:36,219 --> 00:00:38,472
We study the press that printed it.

10
00:00:38,472 --> 00:00:47,166
We count every sign, hunt for patterns, look for a crib, test the people who claimed to crack it, and check whether it is even genuine.

11
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:51,726
Then the boldest theory of all: that the message is not human.

12
00:00:51,726 --> 00:00:53,430
Every claim gets a score.

13
00:00:53,430 --> 00:00:54,930
At the end, a verdict.

14
00:00:54,930 --> 00:00:56,566
Let's open the notebook.

15
00:01:00,899 --> 00:01:01,575
Page one.

16
00:01:01,575 --> 00:01:02,625
The intercept.

17
00:01:02,625 --> 00:01:14,700
In southern Crete, on a ridge above the Mesara plain, stand the ruins of Phaistos, one of the great palaces of the Minoans, the Bronze Age civilisation of Crete.

18
00:01:15,033 --> 00:01:21,469
In 1908 an Italian mission under Federico Halbherr and Luigi Pernier was digging there.

19
00:01:21,469 --> 00:01:29,533
On the third of July, in a group of buildings north-east of the main palace, the disc came out of the ground.

20
00:01:29,866 --> 00:01:38,335
It lay in an underground cell, a kind of basement deposit, in a layer of black earth and ash mixed with burnt cattle bones.

21
00:01:38,335 --> 00:01:43,500
One account says the local foreman spotted it during an evening inspection.

22
00:01:43,833 --> 00:01:51,890
A few centimetres away lay something important: a small clay tablet in Linear A, the everyday script of Minoan Crete.

23
00:01:51,890 --> 00:01:56,366
That tablet is the disc's closest neighbour, and its first alibi.

24
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:58,507
The disc itself is small.

25
00:01:58,507 --> 00:02:05,952
About sixteen centimetres across, up to two centimetres thick, slightly egg-shaped, with rounded edges.

26
00:02:05,952 --> 00:02:09,999
One face is a little concave, the other a little convex.

27
00:02:10,333 --> 00:02:12,298
And it was fired on purpose.

28
00:02:12,298 --> 00:02:18,473
Clay documents in Crete usually survive only because a building burned down around them.

29
00:02:18,473 --> 00:02:23,666
This one was baked deliberately, like pottery, as if it was meant to last.

30
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:32,913
Today it sits behind glass in the Heraklion Archaeological Museum, the most famous object in Minoan archaeology.

31
00:02:32,913 --> 00:02:36,733
A message with no sender, no reader, and no key.

32
00:02:37,066 --> 00:02:37,714
Page two.

33
00:02:37,714 --> 00:02:39,083
How was it written?

34
00:02:39,083 --> 00:02:41,100
Not with a pen, or a stylus.

35
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:49,599
Every sign was pressed into the soft clay with a separate stamp, one stamp for each of the forty-five different signs.

36
00:02:49,933 --> 00:02:57,716
Whoever made it carved a set of punches, probably of metal, then pressed them one after another around a spiral.

37
00:02:57,716 --> 00:03:01,400
The same stamp leaves the same mark, again and again.

38
00:03:01,733 --> 00:03:04,377
That is the principle of movable type.

39
00:03:04,377 --> 00:03:15,233
The typesetter and linguist Herbert Brekle argued that the disc fulfils every definition of a printed text, more than three thousand years before Gutenberg.

40
00:03:15,566 --> 00:03:17,859
Some marks were drawn by hand.

41
00:03:17,859 --> 00:03:20,840
A scored line spirals between the rows.

42
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:24,279
Short radial lines cut the spiral into boxes.

43
00:03:24,279 --> 00:03:29,400
And at the rim, one starting line is marked with five punched dots.

44
00:03:29,733 --> 00:03:35,262
Under some signs there is a tiny slanting stroke, scratched with a stylus.

45
00:03:35,262 --> 00:03:39,745
It appears eighteen times, always on the last sign of a box.

46
00:03:39,745 --> 00:03:40,791
A word ending?

47
00:03:40,791 --> 00:03:42,061
A paragraph mark?

48
00:03:42,061 --> 00:03:43,033
Nobody knows.

49
00:03:43,366 --> 00:03:46,129
And here is the most human detail of all.

50
00:03:46,129 --> 00:03:47,813
The disc has corrections.

51
00:03:47,813 --> 00:03:53,339
In more than a dozen places, a sign was smoothed away and another stamped over it.

52
00:03:53,339 --> 00:03:55,900
Someone made mistakes, and fixed them.

53
00:03:56,233 --> 00:03:57,425
Keep that in mind.

54
00:03:57,425 --> 00:04:01,994
A printing press, a spiral layout, and a maker who sometimes slipped.

55
00:04:01,994 --> 00:04:06,099
These are the fingerprints we will check every theory against.

56
00:04:06,433 --> 00:04:07,221
Page three.

57
00:04:07,221 --> 00:04:07,937
Inventory.

58
00:04:07,937 --> 00:04:10,302
A codebreaker starts by counting.

59
00:04:10,302 --> 00:04:14,816
The disc carries two hundred and forty-two stamped impressions.

60
00:04:14,816 --> 00:04:20,333
One is too damaged to identify, so most counts say two hundred and forty-one.

61
00:04:20,666 --> 00:04:29,473
They are grouped into sixty-one boxes, thirty-one on one side and thirty on the other, each holding between two and seven signs.

62
00:04:29,473 --> 00:04:33,533
Scholars call them words, though nobody can prove they are.

63
00:04:33,866 --> 00:04:38,972
Arthur Evans numbered the forty-five signs, and the nicknames stuck.

64
00:04:38,972 --> 00:04:40,023
A walking man.

65
00:04:40,023 --> 00:04:42,275
A head with a feathered crest.

66
00:04:42,275 --> 00:04:43,327
A shaven head.

67
00:04:43,327 --> 00:04:49,033
A woman, a child, a bow, a shield, a ship, a fish, a bee, a beehive, a tree.

68
00:04:49,366 --> 00:04:53,167
The most common is the plumed head, nineteen times.

69
00:04:53,167 --> 00:04:58,980
Then a cap-like sign, eighteen times, and a round shield with dots, seventeen.

70
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:01,066
Nine signs appear only once.

71
00:05:01,399 --> 00:05:03,191
Now the first real clue.

72
00:05:03,191 --> 00:05:06,326
Alphabets use about twenty to forty signs.

73
00:05:06,326 --> 00:05:10,432
Scripts built on word-signs need hundreds or thousands.

74
00:05:10,432 --> 00:05:15,433
Syllabaries, where each sign is a syllable, usually sit in between.

75
00:05:15,766 --> 00:05:27,061
Forty-five different signs in such a short text fits a syllabary best, perhaps with a few word-signs mixed in, like the other scripts of the Bronze Age Aegean.

76
00:05:27,061 --> 00:05:30,399
The full set may have held fifty-five or sixty.

77
00:05:30,733 --> 00:05:32,245
Which way does it run?

78
00:05:32,245 --> 00:05:36,577
Evans first thought from the centre out, then changed his mind.

79
00:05:36,577 --> 00:05:42,833
Today most agree: start at the five dots on the rim, and read clockwise, spiralling inward.

80
00:05:43,166 --> 00:05:50,888
In 2008 the forty-five signs were even added to Unicode, the standard behind every computer font.

81
00:05:50,888 --> 00:05:53,356
You can type the Phaistos Disc.

82
00:05:53,356 --> 00:05:55,266
You just cannot read it.

83
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:56,329
Page four.

84
00:05:56,329 --> 00:05:56,985
Patterns.

85
00:05:56,985 --> 00:06:01,580
Codebreakers look for what repeats, and the disc repeats a lot.

86
00:06:01,580 --> 00:06:06,612
Of the seventeen shields, thirteen come straight after a plumed head.

87
00:06:06,612 --> 00:06:08,800
That pair opens box after box.

88
00:06:09,133 --> 00:06:15,737
On one side, a four-sign word, plumed head, shield, a bird, a horn, appears three times.

89
00:06:15,737 --> 00:06:20,990
A longer six-sign phrase repeats twice, and so does a seven-sign word.

90
00:06:20,990 --> 00:06:22,866
Like a refrain in a song.

91
00:06:23,199 --> 00:06:25,785
On the other side the habits change.

92
00:06:25,785 --> 00:06:30,813
Many words end in the same few signs, like lines of a poem that rhyme.

93
00:06:30,813 --> 00:06:35,266
The two sides share many signs, but follow different patterns.

94
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:42,135
Refrains and rhymes are what you expect from a hymn, a prayer, or a ritual formula.

95
00:06:42,135 --> 00:06:51,033
The archaeologist Giorgia Baldacci argues the repetitions point to a religious or magical text, made for display.

96
00:06:51,366 --> 00:06:57,712
That would fit a deliberately fired object, found in what Pernier called a temple deposit.

97
00:06:57,712 --> 00:07:00,180
But a list of names can repeat too.

98
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:03,001
Patterns tell us the text is structured.

99
00:07:03,001 --> 00:07:04,199
Not what it says.

100
00:07:04,533 --> 00:07:09,644
Some see a board game, like snakes and ladders, with the boxes as squares.

101
00:07:09,644 --> 00:07:13,443
Others see a calendar, counting days around the spiral.

102
00:07:13,443 --> 00:07:16,966
Each idea fits a few features and ignores the rest.

103
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:19,898
Here is the hard truth of page four.

104
00:07:19,898 --> 00:07:22,785
Two hundred and forty-one signs is tiny.

105
00:07:22,785 --> 00:07:27,766
The statistics that crack a long message have almost nothing to grip.

106
00:07:28,100 --> 00:07:28,855
Page five.

107
00:07:28,855 --> 00:07:30,291
Looking for a crib.

108
00:07:30,291 --> 00:07:33,465
Every great decipherment had outside help.

109
00:07:33,465 --> 00:07:40,266
The Rosetta Stone gave Egyptian hieroglyphs a Greek translation, carved right beside them.

110
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:45,697
Crete had two scripts of its own: Cretan hieroglyphs and Linear A.

111
00:07:45,697 --> 00:07:47,859
Both are still undeciphered.

112
00:07:47,859 --> 00:07:52,879
A later script, Linear B, was cracked in 1952 by Michael Ventris.

113
00:07:52,879 --> 00:07:56,199
It turned out to be an early form of Greek.

114
00:07:56,533 --> 00:08:03,285
But Ventris had thousands of tablets, and the patient grids of Alice Kober before him.

115
00:08:03,285 --> 00:08:05,091
The disc is one object.

116
00:08:05,091 --> 00:08:09,566
In 1948 Kober wrote that very little can be done with it.

117
00:08:09,899 --> 00:08:18,227
The best crib so far is a bronze double axe, found in 1934 in a cave at Arkalochori in central Crete.

118
00:08:18,227 --> 00:08:20,288
It carries fifteen signs.

119
00:08:20,288 --> 00:08:24,987
Some resemble the disc's signs, including a crested head.

120
00:08:24,987 --> 00:08:26,966
None is a perfect match.

121
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:31,671
Then smaller clues turned up at Phaistos itself.

122
00:08:31,671 --> 00:08:39,867
A bowl excavated in 1965 carries a potter's mark almost identical to the disc's comb sign.

123
00:08:39,867 --> 00:08:44,966
A clay sealing found in 1955 shows a very similar motif.

124
00:08:45,299 --> 00:08:53,165
And Phaistos potters had a habit: they decorated fine cups by pressing small stamps into the clay in rows.

125
00:08:53,165 --> 00:09:00,066
Alessandro Sanavia found close parallels for six of the disc's signs on that stamped pottery.

126
00:09:00,399 --> 00:09:02,443
So the disc is not an orphan.

127
00:09:02,443 --> 00:09:06,458
Its technique, and some of its signs, belong to Phaistos.

128
00:09:06,458 --> 00:09:10,122
What we still lack is one word we can actually read.

129
00:09:10,122 --> 00:09:13,433
Without that, every crib is only a resemblance.

130
00:09:13,766 --> 00:09:14,392
Page six.

131
00:09:14,392 --> 00:09:15,365
The solutions.

132
00:09:15,365 --> 00:09:20,091
People have claimed to crack the disc for more than a hundred years.

133
00:09:20,091 --> 00:09:22,524
The problem is not too few answers.

134
00:09:22,524 --> 00:09:23,566
It is too many.

135
00:09:23,899 --> 00:09:32,494
In 1911 George Hempl read it as early Ionic Greek: a prophetess dedicating spoils, with calls for silence.

136
00:09:32,494 --> 00:09:39,466
The same year Florence Stawell read it as Homeric Greek, starting from the other side.

137
00:09:39,799 --> 00:09:46,093
In 1988 Steven Roger Fischer read Greek again, opening with the words hear ye.

138
00:09:46,093 --> 00:09:49,159
Kjell Aartun found a Semitic language.

139
00:09:49,159 --> 00:09:54,000
Others found Hittite, Egyptian, even a route through Basque.

140
00:09:54,333 --> 00:10:08,799
In 2004 a team based in the Netherlands read it as Luwian, a language of Anatolia: a document about land, addressed to Nestor, the king from Homer, naming Phaistos and Knossos.

141
00:10:09,133 --> 00:10:24,500
And in 2014 linguist Gareth Owens, working with the Oxford phonetician John Coleman, presented the disc as a prayer to a Minoan mother goddess, built around a word he read as ee kweh koo ree ah.

142
00:10:24,833 --> 00:10:27,564
Headlines said the code was cracked.

143
00:10:27,564 --> 00:10:29,233
Specialists disagreed.

144
00:10:29,233 --> 00:10:34,240
Owens borrowed sound values from Linear A, which is itself unread.

145
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:39,399
Brent Davis called the readings a stack of unverifiable assumptions.

146
00:10:39,733 --> 00:10:43,195
Elizabeth Barber summed up the problem in one line.

147
00:10:43,195 --> 00:10:52,766
With so few signs, there is not enough data for anyone to prove a decipherment, and by the same token, not enough for anyone to disprove one.

148
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:58,859
That is why the disc can be Greek, Luwian, Semitic and a prayer all at once.

149
00:10:58,859 --> 00:11:02,951
When one text supports every answer, it supports none.

150
00:11:02,951 --> 00:11:03,784
Deciphered?

151
00:11:03,784 --> 00:11:04,466
Unproven.

152
00:11:04,799 --> 00:11:05,691
Page seven.

153
00:11:05,691 --> 00:11:08,121
Is the intercept even genuine?

154
00:11:08,121 --> 00:11:19,299
In 2008 Jerome Eisenberg, an expert on faked antiquities, argued in his journal Minerva that the disc was a modern hoax, made for Pernier.

155
00:11:19,633 --> 00:11:24,681
His case: Pernier envied the spectacular finds of his rivals on Crete.

156
00:11:24,681 --> 00:11:28,070
The disc was too neat, too perfect, too unique.

157
00:11:28,070 --> 00:11:34,633
And it has never been dated by thermoluminescence, which measures when clay was last fired.

158
00:11:34,966 --> 00:11:36,578
That last point is fair.

159
00:11:36,578 --> 00:11:46,653
Greek authorities have not allowed the test, and the writer Andrew Robinson, who does not back the hoax theory, says a lab date would be very welcome.

160
00:11:46,653 --> 00:11:48,399
Until then, there is none.

161
00:11:48,733 --> 00:11:52,983
But the hoax theory has a time-machine problem.

162
00:11:52,983 --> 00:11:59,765
The comb sign turns up on a sealing found in 1955 and a bowl found in 1965.

163
00:11:59,765 --> 00:12:05,733
A forger in 1908 could not copy marks that were still underground.

164
00:12:06,066 --> 00:12:13,940
Add the Linear A tablet in the same layer, the stamped pottery of Phaistos, and those careful corrections.

165
00:12:13,940 --> 00:12:17,358
Why fix mistakes in a text that means nothing?

166
00:12:17,358 --> 00:12:19,958
A 2008 symposium rejected the hoax.

167
00:12:19,958 --> 00:12:21,666
Genuine, very probably.

168
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:22,810
Page eight.

169
00:12:22,810 --> 00:12:24,358
The alien hypothesis.

170
00:12:24,358 --> 00:12:36,666
On the TV series Ancient Aliens, in an episode called The Alien Disks, a guest suggested the disc is a kind of computer disk, meant to be read by a machine from space.

171
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:43,668
Others call it an interstellar navigation chart, a message from the galaxy, even a stargate.

172
00:12:43,668 --> 00:12:49,466
The spiral, they say, looks like a galaxy, or the path of a craft through space.

173
00:12:49,799 --> 00:12:53,410
So read the evidence the way a codebreaker would.

174
00:12:53,410 --> 00:12:55,327
What does the sender know?

175
00:12:55,327 --> 00:12:56,653
Bees and beehives.

176
00:12:56,653 --> 00:12:57,169
A fish.

177
00:12:57,169 --> 00:12:57,685
A ship.

178
00:12:57,685 --> 00:13:03,433
Trees and plants, animal hides, a bull's leg, people with Bronze Age headgear.

179
00:13:03,766 --> 00:13:06,426
That is not a picture of the galaxy.

180
00:13:06,426 --> 00:13:09,086
It is a picture of Bronze Age Crete.

181
00:13:09,086 --> 00:13:15,366
The maker used a Cretan stamping craft, Cretan motifs, and fixed their typos by hand.

182
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:22,273
An interstellar message, stamped in local clay, with typos, buried beside a Linear A tablet?

183
00:13:22,273 --> 00:13:27,775
The alien version explains none of that, and adds a sender who left no trace.

184
00:13:27,775 --> 00:13:28,633
No evidence.

185
00:13:28,966 --> 00:13:29,506
Debrief.

186
00:13:29,506 --> 00:13:31,666
Time to score the Phaistos Disc.

187
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:37,156
A genuine Minoan object, from around seventeen hundred B C: evidence.

188
00:13:37,156 --> 00:13:42,313
Stamped with forty-five punches, an early kind of printing: evidence.

189
00:13:42,313 --> 00:13:45,452
A real script, probably syllables: likely.

190
00:13:45,452 --> 00:13:48,366
A hymn or a prayer: possible, but open.

191
00:13:48,700 --> 00:13:52,103
Deciphered, by any of the claimants: unproven.

192
00:13:52,103 --> 00:13:56,691
A modern forgery: unlikely, though a lab date would settle it.

193
00:13:56,691 --> 00:13:59,799
An alien message or star map: no evidence.

194
00:14:00,133 --> 00:14:00,986
The verdict.

195
00:14:00,986 --> 00:14:02,407
Unbroken, but human.

196
00:14:02,407 --> 00:14:10,154
The Phaistos Disc is a real Minoan text, printed with stamps more than three thousand years before Gutenberg.

197
00:14:10,154 --> 00:14:11,718
We cannot read it yet.

198
00:14:11,718 --> 00:14:16,266
One more inscription in the same script could change everything.

199
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:25,980
Somewhere in Phaistos, around seventeen hundred B C, a person pressed a stamp into wet clay, smoothed out a mistake, and pressed again.

200
00:14:25,980 --> 00:14:28,203
That person is the real mystery.

201
00:14:28,203 --> 00:14:31,399
And one day, a trowel may give us their voice.

202
00:14:31,733 --> 00:14:33,758
Next case: December 1980.

203
00:14:33,758 --> 00:14:41,130
Strange lights among the pines of Rendlesham Forest, right beside two air bases in England.

204
00:14:41,130 --> 00:14:42,588
Britain's Roswell.

205
00:14:42,588 --> 00:14:45,100
What did the airmen really see?

206
00:14:45,433 --> 00:14:47,355
What do you think the disc says?

207
00:14:47,355 --> 00:14:52,399
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
